fibqc verify              — algebra self-check (F/R symbols, S/T, braid unitarity)
fibqc compile <gates>     — compile a circuit over H T S X to a braid word
fibqc compile <gates> <n> — same, with gate net depth n (default 10, max 12)
fibqc jones <gens...>    — Jones polynomial; strands implied by the word
fibqc knot [name]        — Jones value for a knot from the census
fibqc winding            — the phase lattice, in windings
fibqc readout <a> <N>    — one-shot topological readout (ModExp invariant -> winding -> period)
fibqc alkahest <a> <N>   — the four-name dissolution report (root, fixed point, one, ⊡-promotion)
fibqc protocol           — show the IMASM braiding protocol report
fibqc braid <gens...>    — δ: compile a braid word to an IMASM program
fibqc braid <gens...> close — same, closed (trace closure)
fibqc tangle <program>   — μ: read an IMASM program back as a braid word

The circuit compiles as ONE unitary, so the error is incurred once
rather than accumulating gate by gate. Several braid words sit at the
same distance from the target; each is followed as a separate arm, then
the arms that lost compile the residual left by the arm that won.
Net memory, measured in-kernel: 1.7 MB at depth 10, 6.9 MB at depth 12,
against an 8 MB bump arena. Depth 12 peaks at 8156 KB, a 36 KB margin,
so it is the hard ceiling. The command reports its own high-water mark.
Halting. μ∘δ=id.
[SHUTDOWN] μ∘δ=id. Goodbye.
